        "id": "ch7_2_6-1",
        "text": "Exploiting Jenkins\nJenkins exploitation is an excellent example that you can apply to other web\nportals. Once you see a web portal, you need to go over the following checklist:\nI\nI Bypass the login page.\nI\nI Brute-force your way in\nI\nI SQL injection authentication bypass (we\u2019ll cover this one later in the\nbook)\nI\nI Steal the credentials\nI\nI Log in and exploit the weaknesses of the portal functionalities.\nI\nI Execute commands\nI\nI Upload a webshell (we\u2019ll cover that later in this book)\nI\nI If none of these items is feasible, maybe you can try to look for a public\nexploit. Ideally, the administrator did not upgrade the system and is still\nusing an old version.\n(continued)\nChapter 7 I Exploitation Phase\n191\nIf you recall from the enumeration phase, we were able to get a valid account\n(admin:admin) using Hydra. Let\u2019s use it to log in to the portal (see Figure 7.21).\nOur goal at this stage is to get a remote shell, so we will need to execute com-\nmands through this portal. First, let\u2019s start a listener on our Kali host using netcat :\nroot@kali:~# nc -nlvp 1111\nlistening on [any] 1111 ...\nGoing back to the Jenkins page, click the Create New Jobs link to schedule a\ncommand. When the page is loaded, enter a project name, then select the Free-\nstyle project from the list, and finally, click OK (see Figure 7.22).\nFigure 7.21: Jenkins Homepage\nFigure 7.22: Jenkins - New Project\n192\nChapter 7 I Exploitation Phase\nOnce you\u2019re on the project configuration page, scroll down to the Build\nsection and select the Execute Shell menu item (see Figure 7.23).\nWe know from the enumeration phase that this host is Linux based, so we\nwill use Perl to execute the reverse shell command (remember that we have a\nlistener on our Kali host on port 1111). We won\u2019t execute netcat because this",
